    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In this study we report on the occurrence of compaction bands, both parallel to and at high-angles to bedding, from two areas in the aeolian Aztec sandstone exposed in the Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada. Field observation clearly shows that cross-bed domains containing different structural assemblages occur adjacent to each other. Three categories of cross-bed domains are observed: 1) cross-bed domains with bed-parallel compaction bands 2) cross bed domains with high-angle compaction bands, and 3) cross-bed domains with bed-parallel and high-angle compaction bands. The field data show that the orientation of the cross-beds for each domain falls into a certain range. This implies that cross-bed orientation plays a significant role in the formation of compaction bands of different orientations. The relationship between structures of sub-dunes and systematic distribution of compaction bands is also documented. Based on the age relationship between compaction bands of various orientations and their spatial distributions, we propose that more than one set of compaction bands observed in this study accommodated dominantly localized compaction oblique to the principal planes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
